 {head:`<h3>Read the passage, then answer questions 1 to 6</h3><p>For three years the rains failed. The dam outside the village shrank to a cracked brown plate, and the cattle grew so thin that their ribs showed like the bars of a cage. Thabo's grandfather, who had farmed the land for sixty years, said little. Each evening he walked to the edge of the dam and looked at the sky as if it owed him an answer.</p><p>Then, one afternoon in November, the wind changed. Dark clouds piled up behind the hills like a huge grey army. The first drops fell, fat and heavy, raising little puffs of dust. Thabo ran outside, laughing, and held his face up to the sky. For the first time in months, his grandfather smiled.</p>`, qs:[
  M("How long had the drought lasted?","three years",["three months","sixty years","one year"],"'For three years the rains failed.'"),
  M("\"The dam … shrank to a cracked brown plate\" is an example of …","a metaphor",["a simile","onomatopoeia","alliteration"],"The dam is called a plate without 'like' or 'as'."),
  M("\"Their ribs showed like the bars of a cage\" tells us the cattle were …","very thin",["very strong","sick with fever","locked in a cage"],"You could see their ribs."),
  M("Why did the grandfather look at the sky each evening?","He was hoping for rain.",["He was watching birds.","He was counting stars.","He was afraid of storms."],"He looked 'as if it owed him an answer'."),
  M("\"Dark clouds piled up … like a huge grey army\" suggests the clouds were …","big, powerful and coming closer",["small and harmless","white and fluffy","moving away"],"An army is large and advancing."),
  M("How did the mood change at the end of the passage?","from worry and hardship to joy and relief",["from joy to sadness","from anger to fear","it did not change"],"Thabo laughed and the grandfather smiled.")]},
 {head:`<h3>Read the passage, then answer questions 1 to 6</h3><p>Plastic is everywhere. It wraps our bread, carries our shopping and protects our phones. It is cheap, light and strong, which is exactly why it is such a problem. A plastic bottle can take up to 450 years to break down. Every year millions of tonnes of plastic end up in the ocean, where turtles mistake bags for jellyfish and seabirds feed bottle caps to their chicks.</p><p>The good news is that small changes make a difference. Carrying a reusable bag, refusing plastic straws and recycling bottles all reduce waste. In 2004 South Africa introduced a levy on plastic shopping bags, and shoppers began bringing their own. If each of us does a little, together we can do a lot.</p>`, qs:[
  M("According to the passage, why is plastic such a problem?","It is cheap, light and strong, so it is used everywhere and lasts very long.",["It is too expensive.","It breaks down too quickly.","It is too heavy to carry."],"Its useful qualities make it a pollution problem."),
  M("How long can a plastic bottle take to break down?","up to 450 years",["45 days","4,5 years","45 years"],"The passage gives 450 years."),
  M("Why do turtles eat plastic bags?","They mistake them for jellyfish.",["They like the taste.","They are fed by people.","They use them for nests."],"Stated in paragraph 1."),
  M("What is the purpose of this passage?","to inform readers and persuade them to reduce plastic waste",["to advertise plastic bags","to tell a funny story","to describe a holiday"],"It gives facts and calls for action."),
  M("What happened after the levy on plastic bags was introduced?","Shoppers began bringing their own bags.",["Plastic bags became free.","Shops closed.","People stopped shopping."],"Stated in paragraph 2."),
  M("\"If each of us does a little, together we can do a lot.\" This sentence …","encourages readers to act together",["blames readers","gives a statistic","describes a turtle"],"A persuasive conclusion.")]},
 {head:`<h3>Read the passage, then answer questions 1 to 6</h3><p>Nomsa hated the school bus. Every morning she sat alone at the back while the other girls giggled at the front. She pretended to read, but the words swam on the page.</p><p>One Monday a new girl climbed on board. She looked around nervously, clutching her bag like a shield, and every seat was taken except the one next to Nomsa. "Can I sit here?" she asked quietly. Nomsa nodded. By the time the bus reached school, they had discovered that they both loved drawing, both hated maths and both had a little brother who never stopped talking. That afternoon, for the first time, Nomsa couldn't wait for the bus ride home.</p>`, qs:[
  M("Why did Nomsa hate the school bus at first?","She sat alone and felt left out.",["The bus was always late.","The driver was rude.","She got carsick."],"The other girls giggled at the front."),
  M("\"The words swam on the page\" shows that Nomsa …","couldn't concentrate because she was unhappy",["was reading in the water","had very good eyesight","was enjoying her book"],"She was only pretending to read."),
  M("\"Clutching her bag like a shield\" shows that the new girl felt …","nervous and wanted to protect herself",["angry and ready to fight","bored","excited and proud"],"A shield is used for protection."),
  M("Which is NOT something the girls had in common?","They both loved maths.",["They both loved drawing.","They both had a little brother.","They both hated maths."],"They both hated maths."),
  M("What is the main theme of the story?","friendship and the end of loneliness",["the importance of maths","bus safety","sibling rivalry"],"Nomsa finds a friend."),
  M("How did Nomsa's feelings change by the end?","She looked forward to the bus ride instead of hating it.",["She hated the bus even more.","She decided to walk to school.","She felt nothing at all."],"'Couldn't wait for the bus ride home.'")]}
